conv_id=85711 | prompt="
CONVERSATION:
bb21c722adad611b63dfb48ec99e8350: first question on http://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/www-dom/2010JanMar/0010.html
what do you mean by [[ &quot;event type&quot; Event type doesn't act..." | raw="
CONVERSATION:
bb21c722adad611b63dfb48ec99e8350: first question on http://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/www-dom/2010JanMar/0010.html
what do you mean by [[ &quot;event type&quot; Event type doesn't actually say anything about attributes or interfaces. ]]
since an event type is part of a particular interface, isn't it fair (if not precise) to describe it that way?
227a9a17d1db3f8e5c71a150345fb2a6: just a second
227a9a17d1db3f8e5c71a150345fb2a6: so event type
I assume that mean event.type
right?
or it does, based on the &quot;click&quot; example
the fact that event's type is &quot;click&quot; doesn't tell anything about which interface the object implements
a script may create event using var e = document.createEvent(&quot;MutationEvent&quot;); e.initEvent(&quot;click&quot;, true, true); target.dispatchEvent(e);
ok
I know it is a bit tricky to define this all
since by default click is in fact a MouseEvent
yeah, it's a tradeoff between being precise in covering all the cases while being meaningful to authors for the general case
bb21c722adad611b63dfb48ec99e8350: about your &quot;Feature detection doesn't work in all cases&quot; comment
I have 2 possible solutions
1) authors can test for something by Interface name, something like &quot;Events.ProgressEvent.load&quot;
2) give them finer control over testing for specific attributes &quot;Events.mousemove.buttons&quot;
and the use case is?
even for the 1) ?
did you read also G.Smith's emails?
people have expressed a desire to do do feature detection, and I agree with that use case
and hasFeature is already defined and implemented
that is there sure
yes, I've read all of G.Smith's emails
1) looks ok, I think
I don't quite understand 2)
should that be Events.MouseEvent.mousemove.buttons?
but if mousemove is a MouseEvent, it has .buttons
his claim that &quot;browsers lie&quot; is not convincing to me... a) my proposal actually provides a better fine-grained mechanism than was previously defined, and b) even if his feature detection method were implemented, it would still not account for implementation bugs
that is true
though he does have the point that having some method in each eventtarget would allow more fine grained detection
bb21c722adad611b63dfb48ec99e8350: I'll go with (1) then
but yet, that isn't really working
if extensions / user scripts / whatever dispatch events
1) doesn't handle extensions either
again, tricky case
bb21c722adad611b63dfb48ec99e8350: that's why I thought of (2)
look at CSSOM Views
it extends the MouseEvent interface with new attributes
so you could test on those attributes
ah, you mean that
but say that some webapp wants tripleclick
and the browser doesn't support it
(I used &quot;.buttons&quot; because that is something new in D3E, which could be used to test for support of that feature specifically
yet some extension or greasemonkey script can add the support to browser
or would testing Events.MouseEvent.tripleclick just check that if it is possible to create such event
or does it check whether the implementation actually does dispatch such events?
well, a good extensibility mechanism in the browser would let an extension &quot;register&quot; the new features added
good question... 2 answers
greasemonkey scripts are a bit different
1) I think that would be implementation-dependent
they are closer to javascript: bookmarks
which just run something in the page context
2) even if the system isn't perfect, and doesn't catch every case, it can still be useful for the majority of cases
the thing is that I don't want webapps to rely on information which might lack something (the support provided by greasemonkey scripts etc)
fwiw, ProgressEvent.load doesn't seem to be much different than the DOM3 Events load, though it's hard to tell because the events aren't very well defined
ProgressEvent.load is pretty well defined
at least when dispatched to an XHR object
ah, I was looking at http://dev.wf5, age: age."Reasoning: age: age."Reasoning: age: age."Reasoning: age: i age."Reasoning: age: age."Reasoning: age: false, age: age."Reasoning: age: i age."Reasoning: age: false, age: age."Reasoning: age: age."Reasoning: age: age."Reasoning: age: false, age: age."Reasoning: age: age."Reasoning: age: age."Reasoning: age: false, age: age."Reasoning: age: age."Reasoning: age:"")
