as ..." | raw="
CONVERSATION:
bddfdb28870c5800d938fa0993c46d65, can you review http://tc.labs.opera.com/css/namespaces/syntax-012.xml ?
I wonder why it fails in Gecko. I thought they had a pretty good CSS parser
as in, the one that actually matches the specification
Can it wait half an hour?
sure
By fail you mean it doesn't apply the CSS?
it applies some CSS, but not the CSS it needs to apply to pass
trackbot
Hmm, all my browsers seem to apply the CSS. That's strange, the doc is sent as appplication/xml but there is no &lt;?xml-stylesheet?&gt; anywhere...
hmm, my IRC client is acting weird
ah, it's ok again
bddfdb28870c5800d938fa0993c46d65, namespace magic, the question is why it's not green
Namespaces don't have semantics in generic XML, they only separate names in different spaces. It's not said anywhere that html:link is a link, except in XHTML.
can we place leave that discussion on the side?
(HTML5 actually defines that, I think)
Sure, but please don't make tests that rely on sniffing.
The Web would be a rather less useful place if we generalized that awful concept.
(it's not sniffing)
(it's namespace dispatching while parsing)
Whatever it is, it is counter to the XML standard.
not really, although it's true that nobody has written down the details
Why don't you send the doc as XHTML? Then it has semantics.
I guess I'll ask someone else to review the test :)
Another thing that is wrong is the missing attribute on the &lt;style&gt; attribute. As far as I know, TYPE is required.
not in HTML5
Namespace b is undefined. The url() contains invalid characters.
The document looks even less like HTML5 than like XHTML :-)
(Also, we can't make tests in HTML5, because HTML5 doesn't exist yet.)
what invalid characters?
The single quotes.
url('test') is correct, no?
it's just preceded and followed by a comment, or is that not ok?
No comments inside tokens :-) We just clarified that text in the spec last month.
(It said comments may occur *between&quot; tokens, it now says *outside* tokens.
)
and url() is a token?
k
Yes, unlike attr(), e.g.
is url() inside background-image a token as well?
s/inside/as value of/
Yes, same token. Tokenization is independent of property syntax.
weird
(and also understandable)
whoa, changing that makes the whole thing apply
wonders if that's the correct error handling
thanks bddfdb28870c5800d938fa0993c46d65, I suppose I should now figure out what url(/**/''/**/) should do even though it is non-conforming
Haven't looked closely, but I'd say that the error recovery would just skip to the next &quot;;&quot; and continue parsing normally at @namespace c...
that does not appear to be what is happening in e.g. Opera
and what would the url() value be in that scenario?
There would be none. The line reads as ATKEYWORD(@namespace) COMMENT IDENT(b) COMMENT FUNCTION (&quot;url(&quot;) COMMENT STRING('test-b') COMMENT ')' ';'
Which is well-formed, but doesn't mean anything.
if it can be tokenized as such, why are comments disallowed there? (I suppose I should've followed the discussion...)
Maybe Opera ignores the last line because it doesn't kow what &quot;b|&quot; means?
It often can't be tokenized. URLs may contain characters that are otherwise not allowed in CSS, such as url(abc&lt;&lt;&lt;def)
Or better example: url(abc[[[def) which has non-balanced brackets.
Or, which is actually likely to occur: url(path?q1=a;q2=b) with a semicolon.
(removing b|test didn't change anything)
(one of the first things I tried)
Adding a valid declaration of b does cause Opera to apply the green.
sure
url(abc[[[def) is acceptable?
wow
And so does removing &quot;, b|test&quot;
CSS syntax is even more silly than I thought
really? if you keep the invalid rule around? that does not work for me
In CSS1, we didn't need quotes around URLs, the parens of url() were enough.
No, without the invalid rule. Haven't tried putting that back yet.
right

