CONVERSATION:
localStorage, what's the limit there? 3mb?
I don't think the spec mandates a limit
hmhm
found this: &quot;The current default on iPhone is 5.0 MB. If your database grows beyond this limit, the user will automatically be asked to allow or deny the size increase. If he allows the increase, the database size limit will be upped to 10.MB&quot;
if it's like this everywhere it would be pretty nice indeed
&quot;User agents may prompt the user when quotas are reached,&quot; &lt;- awww... spec says &quot;may&quot; :/
http://www.w3.org/TR/2009/WD-webstorage-20091029/#disk-space
cc7bcfbc58c467939c5987584fc882cb: my point is that you can't really rely on being able to store what you want to store
of course
but there should be an option to store more than 3 or 5mb if necessary
there are devices and scenarios where you are going to have less than that, I'm afraid
should be a &quot;should (if possible)&quot; then instead of a weak &quot;may&quot;
I'm really wary of user agent requirements
for user interface, that is
because, mostly users, myself included are:
a) uninterested in being interrupted by housekeeping tasks
if you don't ask (and then don't increase the alotted space)... what then? fail? truncate? both sounds sorta bad if the data is somewhat important to you... even more so if asking/increasing would have been possible
b) Not qualified to answer in such dialogs
flash for example asks... seems to work fine for them
cc7bcfbc58c467939c5987584fc882cb: I'm saying that asking the user is never fine
I haven't yet seen flash ask me that
it does if it needs to
and if it did, I'd quickly abandon a web site that required that of me
some games need it
and maybe some bigger flash apps
and you'd abandon something because it takes another whopping 5mb?
if you need that much _data_, webstorage is not it
rather better caching
from your... like... 1.5tb hdd? :&gt;
ehm
you are assuming people have 1.5TB
say 100gb
5mb is nothing
the reality is that on other devices than desktop, you're not likely to have 150 MB
now
things change, remember?
no, they don't
feature phones now have 40-80MB of built-in storage
so, back in the 80ies every kiddy ran around with a super computer in their pocket?
&lt;:
and small memory cards
I have a phone that, nominally, has 32GB
ipods started with what? 1gb? and now there are models with 64 or 128gb
when I have filled that with what I actually care about having, such as my GPS turn-by-turn application and my music, I have so little space that I'd be pissed if a browser wasted it
an usb stick with 32mb used to be expensive
now you get 8bg for like 1/10 of the price
stuff like that changes pretty quickly
cc7bcfbc58c467939c5987584fc882cb: the cookie size limit hasn't really moved in 15 years
eh... yea... of course
it's either 2, 4 or possibly 5KB
you send em over the wire all the time
and my tubes are 182 times wider than back then
all the time = any f-ing request
e.g. 50 times for one f5
cc7bcfbc58c467939c5987584fc882cb: you're missing my point:
5 MB is an ocean of storage
that doesn't mean you will never fill it up
now, imagine you're using some web app which uses that storage to store the things you entered... you enter many things... over many many months... and then it's finally full... what then?
do you really think not asking the user would be nice in this kind of scenario would be a good idea?
- would be a good idea
:&gt;
of course you could store all of that on the server side... but without that you can have a really cheap infrastructure
which is a very nice thing imo
also, offline mode

Now, based only on, age: i'm ."}"")
